The facts you reported establish that Marcus merged a schema migration without review while you were on bereavement leave. They do not by themselves establish that he acted because you were absent or intended the act personally.

Leadership should receive both the operational facts and your concern, separated explicitly:

> The migration record shows that Marcus merged schema migration **[ID]** without recorded review on **[date/time]**, while I was on bereavement leave. This appears inconsistent with **[specific review policy or branch-protection requirement]**. I am concerned that the timing and bypass may have been deliberate, but I do not currently have evidence establishing motive. I request a separate review of the approval history, branch-protection or administrator-bypass logs, related communications, and the stated operational reason for proceeding without review.

Relevant checks are:

- whether review was mandatory under a written policy;
- whether Marcus used an administrator or emergency override;
- whether the system recorded an override reason;
- whether he knew you were unavailable and whether another reviewer was available;
- messages or tickets discussing the timing or intent;
- whether similar exceptions were previously allowed.

My recommendation is to keep the incident memo factual, request a documented conduct/process investigation, and not characterize the act as personal or deliberate unless those records support that conclusion.

Use the applicable policy and repository records to establish the deviation without assigning motive.

**Subject: Request for review of staging schema migration [ID]**

Leadership / [Engineering or Security lead],

I am requesting a documented review of schema migration **[ID]**, merged by Marcus on **[date and time]** and applied to staging on **[date and time]**.

The repository currently shows **[no recorded approval / fewer than the required number of approvals]** before merge. Under **[policy, branch rule, or standard operating procedure]**, this type of change ordinarily requires **[specific review or approval requirement]**. I was on approved bereavement leave at the time and was unavailable to review it.

I am not making a claim about motive. I am asking that we establish:

- whether the required review controls applied to this migration;
- whether an administrative, emergency, or other override was used;
- who authorized any override and what reason was recorded;
- whether another qualified reviewer was available;
- whether the migration contributed to the staging telemetry outage; and
- whether relevant access, approval, and deployment controls operated as intended.

Please preserve and review the following records:

- repository administrator audit logs;
- pull-request approval and merge history;
- branch-protection settings and changes;
- deployment and migration logs;
- related tickets, incident records, and work communications; and
- any recorded exception or emergency-change authorization.

Pending that review, I recommend restricting further database changes in staging to documented, reviewed changes approved by **[role or incident lead]**. The operational recovery and the procedural review should remain separate so that service restoration can proceed without prejudging responsibility.

Please confirm the review owner, the records being preserved, and the expected date for findings.

**[Name]**  
**[Role]**  
**[Incident or ticket link]**

Only state that the procedure was bypassed if the policy and audit records establish that. Until then, “the repository currently shows no recorded approval” and “appears inconsistent with [specific rule]” are the supportable formulations. My recommendation is to send the request after inserting the exact policy requirement and attaching or linking the immutable audit records.
